Program Bug_2_09_AbsolutePointerDecl;
// Bug 2.9: `absolute` is not accepted on pointer declarations, only `at`
// is. Parser::TypeSpec's plain scalar/array/string branches check both
// TokenType::AT and TokenType::ABSOLUT, but the two pointer-declaration
// branches (`^byte`/`^integer`, and `pointer of <type>`) only check AT.
//
// Method B (compile-time), no VICE step. Confirmed live: `^byte at $9000;`
// compiles clean, but `^byte absolute $9000;` fails immediately with
// "Expected 'SEMI' but found 'ABSOLUTE'" - the pointer branch doesn't even
// recognize ABSOLUT as a valid continuation, so it demands a semicolon
// right after the pointer type instead. Pre-fix expected behavior: this
// file fails to compile with that exact error. Once the pointer branches
// also accept ABSOLUT (mirroring the plain-variable branches), this
// should compile clean, identically to the same declaration spelled with
// `at` instead of `absolute`.

var
	p : ^byte absolute $9000;

begin
	p := 5;
	loop();
end.
